From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Andr=C3=A9=20Braga?= <code@andrebraga.com>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 19:27:50 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] soc: qcom: add a CPU memory latency bandwidth voter

On SM8750, Qualcomm's memlat runs in the kernel rather than in CPUCP:
it samples each CPU's instructions, cycles and cache misses, and while
a CPU misses often per instruction it votes the LLCC and DDR frequency
its table maps the CPU frequency to. Mainline has nothing that does
this, so CPU work that waits on memory runs with the buses wherever
other voters leave them.

Add a driver that does it from a DT description in the shape of the
vendor's memlat nodes, so a SoC brings its own monitors, tables and
paths. Misses are perf events: a CPU PMU event, or one from another PMU
the DT points at, such as the LLCC's per-CPU read-miss counter. Each
CPU samples its own counters from a deferrable timer, which rides a
busy CPU's tick and never wakes an idle one; CPUs that did not sample
drop out of the vote, and at a bus's floor the vote is removed. An
enable file in sysfs stops and resumes it.
